<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</h1><p class="st-meta">1043 CE – 1127 CE (approx.) · capital Melfi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Norman duchy of Robert Guiscard, which drove the Byzantines out of southern Italy and laid the groundwork for the Kingdom of Sicily.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iron-arm">William Iron Arm</a> as their count at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> around 1042–1043. After his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogo-of-hauteville">Drogo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-of-hauteville">Humphrey</a>, their half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> took over in 1057, and in 1059 Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> invested him at Melfi as duke of Apulia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and, in prospect, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> the duchy's overlord.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Guiscard</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> in 1071, ending Byzantine rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a> in 1077, which became his capital. The duchy ruled Greek-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, keeping Byzantine officials and Greek monasteries alongside Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Its army also invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> across the Adriatic in 1081, and in 1084 sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> while rescuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Guiscard</a>'s death in 1085, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-borsa">Roger Borsa</a> and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-duke-of-apulia">William II</a> struggled to control rebellious barons. When William died childless in 1127, his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sicily">County of Sicily</a> claimed the duchy, and in 1130 merged it into the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria.jpg" alt="Duchy of Apulia and Calabria" width="320" height="401"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_and_Illyria_1084_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1043 CE – 1127 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Melfi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Guiscard, Roger Borsa, William II of Apulia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Greek Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norman French, Greek, Italo-Romance</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Apulia, Duchy of Apulia</dd></dl></aside></div>
